Q: HTTP/1.1 and HTTP/2 — are those HTTP and HTTPS?

No.

────────────────────────────────────────
Two separate ideas

A) Version of HTTP
- HTTP/1.0
- HTTP/1.1  ← classic REST/Postman default feel
- HTTP/2    ← faster (multiplexing, binary, compressed headers)
- HTTP/3    ← HTTP over QUIC/UDP

B) Secure or not
- HTTP  = plain (`http://`, port 80)
- HTTPS = HTTP + TLS (`https://`, port 443)

────────────────────────────────────────
Combine them

HTTPS is not “HTTP/2”.
HTTPS means encrypted channel; inside it you may speak HTTP/1.1 or HTTP/2.

Browser to Google:
usually HTTPS + HTTP/2 (or HTTP/3)

Local Spring Boot:
often HTTP/1.1 without TLS (`http://localhost:8080`)

────────────────────────────────────────
HTTP/1.1 vs HTTP/2 (quick)

| | HTTP/1.1 | HTTP/2 |
| - | -------- | ------ |
| Format | text | binary frames |
| Many requests | often many connections / head-of-line issues | multiplexing on one connection |
| Headers | repeated, heavier | compressed (HPACK) |
| Typical web use | still everywhere | usually over HTTPS |

────────────────────────────────────────
❓ Does HTTP/2 mean “two-way”?

NO. The “2” only means **version 2**, not two-way communication.

Normal REST on HTTP/1.1 or HTTP/2 is still mostly:
Client request → Server response

What HTTP/2 actually improves:
1) **Multiplexing** — many requests/responses in parallel on **one** TCP connection
2) Binary frames (more efficient)
3) Header compression
4) Optional **server push** (server can push extra resources — limited use today)

So HTTP/2 is “faster HTTP”, not “WebSocket-style always two-way chat.”

Don’t confuse:

| Term | Meaning |
| ---- | ------- |
| HTTP/2 | HTTP version 2 (multiplexed) |
| Bidirectional streaming | Both sides send streams of messages (gRPC feature on HTTP/2) |
| WebSocket | Full-duplex persistent messages over an upgraded connection |

gRPC can do bidirectional streaming **because it uses HTTP/2 features**,
but plain Spring `@GetMapping` / `@PostMapping` over HTTP/2 is still request/response.

────────────────────────────────────────
One-liner

“HTTP/1.1 and HTTP/2 are versions; HTTP vs HTTPS is encryption (TLS). HTTP/2’s 2 ≠ two-way — it means version 2 with multiplexing.”
